<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uganda People's Congress</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as UPC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Uganda People's Congress is a political party founded in 1960 under the leadership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-obote">Milton Obote</a>. In alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-yekka">Kabaka Yekka</a> it formed Uganda's first government at independence in 1962. It ruled until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a>'s coup in 1971, returned to power after the disputed elections of 1980, and was overthrown again in 1985. It drew its support mainly from northern and eastern Uganda.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uganda-peoples-congress.jpg" alt="Uganda People's Congress" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BommSchips · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Uganda_People%27s_Congress.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>As independence approached, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-yekka">Kabaka Yekka</a> (The King Only), the party of the Buganda king, sought wide autonomy for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baganda">Baganda</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> stood for national unity and drew support in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> south outside Buganda. The Uganda National Congress, led by the northerner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-obote">Milton Obote</a> but attracting many southerners, particularly in the east, joined the Uganda People's Union to form the UPC. With A.G. Mehta, Obote represented the party at the Ugandan Constitutional Conference at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancaster-house">Lancaster House</a> in 1961.</p><p>In the first pre-independence election the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedicto-kiwanuka">Benedicto Kiwanuka</a> emerged as the largest party, while the UPC won most seats in the north and east. An alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-yekka">Kabaka Yekka</a>, in which Obote offered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka</a> a ceremonial role and the retention of his royal powers, made Obote prime minister instead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The alliance did not last. In 1966 Obote ordered a military attack on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka</a>'s palace, led by the army officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a>; the Kabaka escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and Obote declared himself President of Uganda. The step began the party's decline as a popular party, and Obote turned increasingly to his northern support. The 1969 elections were cancelled, and in 1971 Amin overthrew the government.</p><p>After Amin's fall in 1979 the UPC returned, closely aligned with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a>. When it was declared winner of the 1980 elections amid widespread suspicion of rigging, Obote made the junta's head, Paulo Muwanga, his vice president, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoweri-museveni">Yoweri Museveni</a> rejected the result and took up arms. The army, traditionally dominated by northerners, carried out numerous atrocities, particularly in the south, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tito-okello">Tito Okello</a> overthrew Obote in 1985.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Those years fixed the UPC's image as a northern party more firmly than ever. Obote remained party head until his death in exile in October 2005. His widow Miria, elected party president on 28 November 2005, won 0.8 percent of the presidential vote in 2006, when the party won nine parliamentary seats. Olara Otunnu, a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> undersecretary-general for children and armed conflict, led the party from 14 May 2010, and Obote's son Jimmy Akena became its president on 2 July 2015.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uganda People's Congress” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At independence in 1962 Buganda received federal status, and its party, Kabaka Yekka, allied with Milton Obote’s <b>Uganda People’s Congress</b>; Mutesa II became president of Uganda in 1963.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
